(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 46-year-old man who has drunk heavily for 20 years has six months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). The apical impulse is displaced laterally and a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows dilated, thin-walled ventricles with an ejection fraction of 25%, and coronary angiography shows no significant narrowing. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Stress cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dilation and impaired contraction without severe coronary disease or pressure or volume overload define dilated cardiomyopathy, and long-term excess alcohol is one of its named causes. The displaced apical impulse and third heart sound fit a dilated, volume-overloaded ventricle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, what happens after a negative D-dimer?","choices":["The test is repeated in a week","Venography is performed","The work-up stops","Duplex ultrasound follows"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the low likelihood Wells category a negative D-dimer ends the work-up, since the test is sensitive enough to rule out deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 4 weeks ago and now feels well. A repeat electrocardiogram shows widespread T-wave inversions without ST elevation, and troponin is normal. Which best explains the tracing?","choices":["A new silent myocardial infarction","Expected evolution of the illness","Hyperkalemia from colchicine","Developing cardiac tamponade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classic evolution is widespread ST elevation with PR depression, then ST normalization with T-wave flattening, then inversion of the flattened T waves, and finally a normal tracing. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a story strongly suggesting constrictive pericarditis: edema, ascites, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration). His chest radiograph shows no pericardial calcification. How should this radiograph be interpreted?","choices":["It does not exclude constriction","It points to restrictive myopathy","It shows the cause is tamponade","It rules out constrictive disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial calcification is highly consistent with constriction when present, but most patients do not have it on chest radiograph, so a film without calcium cannot rule the diagnosis out. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A vagal maneuver is first line for stable supraventricular (narrow complex) tachycardia, before adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy, an ejection fraction of 25% and breathlessness on minimal exertion despite optimal medical therapy has a left bundle branch block with a QRS of 0.16 seconds. Which treatment addresses the conduction problem?","choices":["Biventricular pacemaker","Single-chamber pacemaker","Intravenous magnesium","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, except symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which gets a biventricular pacer. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with painful blue toes and palpable pulses asks what the emboli in his toes are made of. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Clot from the left atrium","Infected valve vegetations","Air from a catheter line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions to the small vessels of skin and muscle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measures are advised for adults with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Strict bed rest and total fluid restriction","Fluid loading and a high-salt diet plan","Caffeine avoidance and deliberate weight gain","Sodium restriction and alcohol cessation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adult lifestyle modifications are sodium restriction and alcohol cessation, alongside guideline-directed medical therapy and device therapy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["A beta or calcium channel blocker","Adenosine twice","Synchronized cardioversion","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; rate control is the first goal, though harder to achieve than in fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has crushing chest pain and breathlessness the day after her husband's funeral. She has no hypertension, diabetes or smoking history. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation with T-wave inversion, troponin is elevated, and echocardiogram shows hypokinesis of the mid and apical segments of the left ventricle. Catheterization shows normal coronary arteries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A postmenopausal woman after an emotional stressor, with ST elevation, raised troponin, mid and apical hypokinesis and normal coronary arteries at catheterization, has Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with a week of fever and myalgia now has severe breathlessness. She is cold and clammy with a blood pressure of 78/50, heart rate 128, raised jugular venous pressure and crackles throughout both lungs. Myocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Exercise testing to grade severity","Aggressive hemodynamic stabilization","Outpatient follow-up in one week","Elective biopsy before any support"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myocarditis can present as cardiogenic shock, and although supportive care is the mainstay, treatment is directed toward the scenario and may require aggressive hemodynamic stabilization first. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, three-part rubbing sound) at the left sternal border. Which drug should be avoided in treating his pericarditis?","choices":["Colchicine","Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Pantoprazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is post-infarction (Dressler) pericarditis, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen are not given after a myocardial infarction; glucocorticoids are also avoided. Aspirin plus colchicine is used. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman taking metoprolol and diltiazem for palpitations has a new rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens until a QRS is dropped. She has mild fatigue. Which part of her history is a named cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Her mild fatigue","Her female sex","Her history of palpitations","Her rate-slowing drugs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, with digoxin, are named causes of Mobitz type I block, so her medication list is the first place to look. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who started amlodipine for hypertension five weeks ago has bilateral, symmetric ankle swelling without warmth, redness or tenderness. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Bilateral leg ultrasound","Review the medication as the cause","Order a D-dimer","Start therapeutic anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine commonly cause edema, typically bilateral and without signs of inflammation. Recognizing drug-induced edema and reviewing the medication list is the appropriate step. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man arrives with a suddenly cold, pale, pulseless left foot. Vascular surgery has been called. Which medical therapy should be started?","choices":["Oral aspirin alone","Intravenous heparin","Cilostazol","Pentoxifylline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The medical therapy for acute peripheral arterial occlusion is anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, started alongside an immediate vascular surgery consult. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man is confused, with a blood pressure of 74/40 and a heart rate of 32. The electrocardiogram shows P waves and wide QRS complexes at two separate regular rates with no relationship between them. Which is the most appropriate immediate measure?","choices":["Defibrillation","Intravenous amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unstable complete heart block is treated emergently with transcutaneous pacing, pads on the chest, as a bridge to the definitive permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with diabetes and known black, shriveled toe tips now has a moist, swollen, blistered forefoot. His temperature is 39.2 C and heart rate 118. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Supervised exercise referral","Emergency surgical consultation","Observe until demarcation","Start cilostazol and recheck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Wet gangrene (moist, edematous, blistering tissue with bacterial infection, often clostridial) is a surgical emergency because of the risk of sepsis, unlike the dry gangrene common in peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man hospitalized for six days with pneumonia develops swelling of the left calf. His team considers using the Wells criteria to estimate his probability of deep vein thrombosis. Which statement about applying the Wells criteria to him is accurate?","choices":["A low score replaces all testing","Designed for upper-extremity clots","Meant for outpatient or emergency use","Most accurate in hospitalized patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Wells criteria are used in the outpatient and emergency department settings, not for inpatients, in whom the score is less useful. A hospitalized patient needs clinical judgment and ultrasound rather than this pathway. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates and long pauses. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fast rhythms alternating with bradycardia and offset pauses are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome in which the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a painful, swollen right groin two days after a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a blood-filled cavity outside the arterial wall, communicating with the lumen. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute limb ischemia","Femoral true aneurysm","Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Atheroembolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm is a communication between the arterial lumen and overlying connective tissue after arterial rupture; the common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, from laceration by the access needle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which anticoagulation strategy is described as the classical, waning approach?","choices":["Heparin bridged to warfarin","Apixaban with a loading period","Oral rivaroxaban with loading","Parenteral therapy, then dabigatran"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classical strategy starts with heparin and bridges to warfarin; it is cheap and reliable but needs frequent laboratory follow-up of the international normalized ratio. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant born at 28 weeks is in the neonatal intensive care unit with a central venous catheter and parenteral nutrition. She develops feeding difficulty, tachycardia, hypotension and a new murmur, and echocardiography shows a right-sided vegetation. Which organism group is particularly tied to her risk factors?","choices":["Enterococci","Candida species","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neonatal fungal endocarditis is tied to central venous catheters, hyperalimentation and neonatal intensive care or prematurity; Staphylococcus aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci are also common. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has frequent, disruptive episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry and wants a definitive cure. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Catheter ablation","Septal myectomy","Valve replacement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation offers a select subset of patients with nodal reentry the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95%. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which electrical treatment is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion","Drugs first, shock later"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable and its QRS shape keeps changing, so it is defibrillated (unsynchronized) even when a pulse is present. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has viridans streptococcal endocarditis diagnosed early, before any heart failure or abscess, and she starts appropriate antibiotics the same day. Which statement about her outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Valve replacement in most","Death within weeks is likely","Highest mortality of any","Relatively good prognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococcal endocarditis has a relatively good prognosis if antibiotic treatment starts before complications occur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which plaque is the vulnerable, unstable type?","choices":["Fatty core, thin fibrous shell","Calcified core, thick media layer","Small core, thick fibrous shell","Fatty streak, no fibrous shell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A vulnerable plaque has a fatty core under a thin fibrous outer shell and can rupture into the bloodstream; clotting then begins at the rupture site and can go on to occlude the artery. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"For recurrent, colchicine-resistant pericarditis with an inflammatory phenotype, which therapy do the 2025 guidelines now support earlier in the course?","choices":["Immediate pericardiectomy","Azathioprine","Intravenous immunoglobulin","An interleukin-1 blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The 2025 guidelines support anti-interleukin-1 therapy (anakinra or rilonacept) earlier in the course with an inflammatory phenotype, and it is preferred for recurrent, colchicine-resistant or steroid-dependent disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a native mitral valve has 5 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue and a new murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have just been drawn, and the organism is not yet known. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral doxycycline for 2 weeks","Nafcillin plus rifampin","Oral amoxicillin, single dose","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci; vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is the regimen for a subacute presentation, native valve endocarditis, or a prosthetic valve in place more than a year. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["Adenosine","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atropine is first line for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a long QT has recurrent runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Between runs he is alert, with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the first treatment?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What defines a transient ischemic attack?","choices":["Headache without any deficit","Global confusion for one week","Focal deficit lasting under 24 hours","Focal deficit lasting over 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack is a focal loss of neurologic function lasting less than 24 hours; it and stroke classify a patient with carotid disease as symptomatic. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which high-risk pediatric condition warrants endocarditis prophylaxis before qualifying procedures?","choices":["A family history of valve disease","A defect repaired years ago, no residual leak","Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease","An innocent murmur found on screening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-risk pediatric conditions include unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease (including palliative shunts and conduits), prosthetic valves or material, prior endocarditis, and cardiac transplant with valvulopathy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous atropine","Oral metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated: nodal blockade leaves the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has chest pressure when climbing stairs. Coronary angiography performed before a planned myectomy shows normal coronary arteries. Which explanation best accounts for his exertional chest pain?","choices":["Unrecognized coronary plaque rupture","Outflow obstruction and thick muscle demand","A dilated, thin-walled ventricle","Inflammation of the pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angina without coronary disease in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy comes from left ventricular outflow tract obstruction and the increased oxygen consumption of hypertrophied muscle, causing ischemia during exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy drinks most of a bottle of whiskey every night. He is on a beta blocker and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, his ejection fraction is 30%, and he asks whether anything he does himself could help his heart recover. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Wine would be safer than spirits for him","Only a transplant could restore function","Stopping alcohol may let function recover","Damage is fixed once the heart dilates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With long-term excess alcohol as the cause, cardiac function can recover if intake is reduced or stopped, and removing the offending source is part of patient education for dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation at 170 has a blood pressure of 74/40 and is drowsy and confused. Which is the most appropriate immediate step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Intravenous rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial fibrillation (hypotension and altered mental status) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has had positive blood cultures for 10 days despite an antibiotic regimen that matches his organism's sensitivities, and he still has fevers. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Switch to oral therapy","Stop antibiotics and observe","Start full anticoagulation","Evaluation for surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy is an indication for surgery, as are heart failure from valve dysfunction, abscess and fungal infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["A side effect of sedation","Low-flow ischemia from heart block","Septic cerebral emboli","A primary bacterial meningitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vegetations shower septic emboli into the systemic circulation; the brain is affected (ischemic stroke, abscess, hemorrhage, meningitis), producing neurologic deficits such as confusion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis is otherwise healthy and hemodynamically stable. The plan is oral anticoagulation monotherapy with a loading phase followed by maintenance, without any injection lead-in. Which drug fits this strategy?","choices":["Apixaban","Fondaparinux","Dabigatran","Edoxaban"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban, a direct factor Xa inhibitor, is given as oral monotherapy with a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, one of the three major anticoagulation strategies. Rivaroxaban follows the same pattern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with known Mobitz type II block suddenly collapses while gardening and regains consciousness within a minute; her husband saw brief jerking of her arms. Her heart rate is 36. Which term describes this event?","choices":["Transient ischemic attack","Absence seizure","Vasovagal syncope","Stokes-Adams attack"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Stokes-Adams attack is a sudden fainting spell or seizure from a rapid, temporary drop in blood flow to the brain, a recognized complication of Mobitz type II block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia with one consistent, abnormally shaped P wave before each QRS, starting abruptly in someone without heart disease under increased metabolic demand, is focal atrial tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with known obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border that radiates to the suprasternal notch. At the bedside, the examiner tests how the murmur responds to changes in blood flow through the heart. Which maneuver would make his murmur louder?","choices":["Passive leg raise","Valsalva maneuver","Rapid squatting","Sustained handgrip"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anything that decreases blood flow in the heart increases the outflow obstruction and the murmur. The Valsalva maneuver and standing reduce preload, so the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy gets louder. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is 4 days into his recovery from a large anterior myocardial infarction when he suddenly becomes hypotensive, with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Free-wall rupture","Massive pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Free-wall rupture after myocardial infarction is a recognized cause of tamponade, alongside acute pericarditis, iatrogenic injury, trauma, malignancy and aortic dissection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion from metastatic cancer reports a hoarse voice, hiccups and difficulty swallowing. Which mechanism explains his hoarseness?","choices":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement","Superior vena cava obstruction","Hypoglossal nerve compression","Direct invasion of the vocal cords"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Large effusions can cause dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, the hoarseness from impingement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Survival without treatment is usually normal","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Age does not influence the outcome of surgery","His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["High potassium, low sodium","Low calcium, high sodium","Low potassium, low magnesium","High phosphate, low chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has a left forearm arteriovenous fistula that was created four months ago. Last month it had a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the access) and a soft bruit. At today's visit no thrill can be felt and no bruit is heard over the access. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Warm compresses and an anti-inflammatory drug","Reassurance with routine follow-up","Urgent referral for access evaluation","Repeat examination in 7 to 10 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A healthy fistula has a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit with systolic and diastolic components. Loss of both means the access is no longer working, so it needs prompt referral to the specialist. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy is found to have cardiac amyloidosis, with amyloid protein deposited in the heart tissue. He has breathlessness, leg edema and ascites. Which electrocardiogram finding is expected with this infiltrative disease?","choices":["Low QRS voltage","ST elevation","Prominent Q waves","Left axis deviation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy may show low QRS voltage in amyloidosis infiltrative disease, and atrial fibrillation and bundle branch blocks are also common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a permanent pacemaker has severe claudication, and an intervention is planned. Which imaging study should be avoided?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography","Conventional angiography","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance angiography is contraindicated with pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips. It is otherwise the alternative to computed tomography angiography in contrast allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and night sweats. Two separate blood cultures grow Streptococcus gallolyticus, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the aortic valve. He has no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Unclassifiable for now","Definite endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two major criteria are met: a typical organism (Streptococcus gallolyticus) in two separate cultures and an oscillating mass on echocardiogram. Two major criteria alone establish definite endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who had a left leg deep vein thrombosis after a hip fracture ten years ago reports a heavy, aching left lower leg by evening that eases when she elevates it. Examination shows pitting edema, shiny taut skin at the ankle and brownish pigmentation. Pedal pulses are strong. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral artery disease","Chronic venous insufficiency","Lymphedema","Acute deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Progressive pitting edema with dull discomfort worse on standing, shiny taut ankle skin and brownish pigmentation after a prior deep vein thrombosis are the classic picture of chronic venous insufficiency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with visible varicose veins asks what symptoms she is most likely to have. Which should she be told?","choices":["Calf cramping that eases with rest","Numb, burning feet at night","Dull, achy heaviness after standing","Sharp pain at rest when lying flat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common symptom of varicose veins is a dull, achy heaviness or fatigue brought on by periods of standing, sometimes with itching over the veins. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions despite cutting out caffeine and alcohol. She has no contraindications to beta blockade. Which treatment is first?","choices":["Procainamide","Warfarin","Metoprolol","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with tamponade from a malignant effusion has a blood pressure of 76/48 while the team prepares for pericardiocentesis. Which measure best supports her in the meantime?","choices":["Intravenous fluid resuscitation","Oral beta blocker now","Intravenous loop diuretic","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous fluid resuscitation increases preload and helps prevent right ventricular collapse; it is a temporizing, stabilizing measure until pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has been on strict bed rest for six days during an admission for pneumonia and now has swelling and aching of the entire left leg. Which component of the Virchow triad does his prolonged bed rest represent?","choices":["Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Arterial vasospasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bed rest is immobilization, which alters blood flow and lets blood pool in the leg veins, the venous stasis component of the Virchow triad. Prolonged immobilization raises the risk of deep vein thrombosis about twenty-fold. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a known ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm develops a new diastolic murmur, exertional dyspnea and leg swelling. Which complication does the aneurysm's location explain?","choices":["Recurrent laryngeal palsy","Esophageal compression","Tracheal compression","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An ascending thoracic aneurysm can cause aortic regurgitation and heart failure, which explains the new diastolic murmur, breathlessness and edema. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Most recover completely","A third develop dilation","Half die within five years","Poorest of heart diseases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The dysfunction is transient, and most patients with stress cardiomyopathy recover completely with supportive care. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm reports early satiety and pain isolated to his right groin, with no abdominal tenderness. Which explains the groin pain?","choices":["A femoral pseudoaneurysm","Renal colic from a stone","An inguinal hernia","Pressure on the femoral nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic unruptured aneurysm can cause isolated groin pain from pressure on the femoral nerve, alongside abdominal, back or flank pain and early satiety as it expands. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 9 days after hip surgery. She develops a new fever, and two sets of blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus; she has no indwelling catheter, and examination finds no wound or other source. Which diagnosis must be suspected?","choices":["Drug fever from antibiotics","Postoperative atelectasis","Contamination of both culture sets","Health care-associated endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Health care-associated endocarditis should be suspected in hospitalized patients who develop fevers and positive blood cultures without an apparent source; Staphylococcus aureus causes about half of these cases. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118. Her electrocardiogram shows a new right bundle branch block. Which acute cause of the block should be suspected?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Hypertensive crisis","Pericardial effusion","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block, and her postoperative setting fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"}]};
